# Diary — <work item name>

> Spec: [`spec.md`](./spec.md) • Progress: [`progress.md`](./progress.md)
> Started: <YYYY-MM-DD>

Chronological narrative of this work item: discoveries, decisions, plan changes, surprises, dead
ends, and resolutions. Append-only — new entries go at the **top** (newest first). Old entries are
not deleted or rewritten; if you're wrong about something, write a new entry correcting it.

> **Reading discipline (keeps reviews and handoffs cheap).** This file grows without bound. Agents
> read the **recent slice** — entries since the last review, or the last ~10 — not the whole history;
> they reach for older entries only when a specific finding needs the backstory. So keep each entry
> short (one paragraph) and self-contained, and capture the *why*, not a diff dump.
>
> **Compaction (mandatory, done by the writer).** Whoever appends an entry counts the `## ` entry
> headings afterwards. If there are **more than 40**, compact in the same edit: move every entry
> *older than the newest 15* whose category is `note`, `discovery`, `unblock`, `blocker`, or
> `review-feedback` **verbatim** to the end of a sibling `diary-archive.md` (create it if absent;
> keep newest-first order), and leave **one** entry in `diary.md` titled
> `Compacted: <oldest date> .. <newest date>` (category `note`) saying how many entries moved and
> pointing at `diary-archive.md`. **Never move a `decision-change` or `plan-change` entry** — they
> stay in `diary.md`, where the recent slice is read. Nothing is deleted; history only leaves the
> default read path. 40 keeps the file to roughly a screenful-per-entry skim; 15 keeps a full
> review cycle of context.

**Always write a diary entry when**:

- A decision in `decisions.md` changes (supersession).
- A phase or iteration shape changes (scope, files touched, acceptance).
- An assumption from `context.md` turns out to be wrong.
- A blocker is hit, and again when it's resolved.
- A review surfaces a finding that affects the plan (not just the code).
- A discovery during coding shifts the spec.

Each entry is short — one paragraph, plain language. Pretend you're writing to a teammate who'll
read this in three months and needs to understand "what happened on this work item."

